+% Generated by roxygen2: do not edit by hand
+% Please edit documentation in R/model_MTLNR.R
+\name{MTLNR}
+\alias{MTLNR}
+\title{The Correlated Multiple-Threshold Log-Normal Race Model}
+\usage{
+MTLNR(n_ratings = 3)
+}
+\arguments{
+\item{n_ratings}{Number of rating categories K (>= 1). With K = 1 the model
+is the binary correlated log-normal race.}
+}
+\value{
+A model list with all the necessary functions for EMC2 to sample.
+}
+\description{
+Model file for the correlated multiple-threshold log-normal race (MTLNR) of
+Reynolds, Kvam, Osth and Heathcote (2020), a model of choice, response time
+and a rating (e.g. confidence) given after the choice.
+}
+\details{
+The data need a factor \code{R} with two levels, \code{rt}, and a numeric column \code{RR}
+holding the rating, an integer from 1 (lowest) to \code{n_ratings} (highest).\tabular{lllll}{
+   \strong{Parameter} \tab \strong{Transform} \tab \strong{Natural scale} \tab \strong{Default} \tab \strong{Interpretation} \cr
+   \emph{m} \tab - \tab [-Inf, Inf] \tab 1 \tab Mean log finishing time \cr
+   \emph{s} \tab log \tab [0, Inf] \tab log(1) \tab SD of log finishing time \cr
+   \emph{t0} \tab log \tab [0, Inf] \tab log(0) \tab Non-decision time \cr
+   \emph{rho} \tab probit on (-1, 1) \tab [-1, 1] \tab 0 \tab Correlation of the log finishing times (trial level, must not depend on lR or lM) \cr
+   \emph{c1} \tab log \tab [0, Inf] \tab log(0.5) \tab First rating criterion, -log of the largest threshold \cr
+   \emph{ck} \tab log \tab [0, Inf] \tab log(0.5) \tab Increment to the next criterion \cr
+}
+
+
+The ratings are read from the losing accumulator's evidence at the decision,
+as a proportion of its threshold, against thresholds
+\verb{d_\{K-k\} = exp(-(c1 + ... + ck))}, so rating \code{c} is given when that proportion
+lies between \verb{d_\{K-c\}} and \verb{d_\{K-c+1\}}. The thresholds belong to the losing
+accumulator, so the criteria are usually mapped \code{~ lR}. The natural-scale
+thresholds \verb{d1 .. d\{K-1\}} are reported with \code{add_recalculated = TRUE}.
+}
+\examples{
+# three ratings is the default; other numbers through a wrapper
+MTLNR2 <- function() MTLNR(n_ratings = 2)
+}
